            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Gujarati for to drive in, then the Gujarati for to spend (time)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: તરફ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "તરફ — taraph — \"towards; in the direction of\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "અમે નદી તરફ ચાલ્યા. ame nadī taraph cālyā. We walked towards the river."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "તરફ (taraph) is usually traced to Arabic ṭaraf, \"a side, an edge\", which came through Persian; after a noun it means towards it: ઘર તરફ, towards home."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for getting around."
            }
          ]
        },
